          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">March 20, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-seal" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why You Should Seal a Pool Deck</a>
          <a href="#types-of-sealers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Wet Look vs. Natural vs. Non-Slip</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#how-to-seal" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. How to Seal a Pool Deck (DIY Guide)</a>
          <a href="#non-slip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Keeping It Non-Slip</a>
          <a href="#salt-water" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Salt Water Pool Considerations</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A pool deck takes more abuse than almost any paved surface around your home: chlorinated splashing, sunscreen and body oils, blazing UV, freeze-thaw cycles, and bare feet all day long. The right paver sealer locks down the joint sand, deepens the color, and repels stains — but the wrong one turns slick when wet or clouds over into an ugly white haze. After a full swim season of testing, here's what actually holds up poolside.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Seal -->
        <div id="why-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why You Should Seal Your Pool Deck Pavers
          </h2>
          <p>
            Unsealed pavers around a pool are constantly under attack. Splashed pool water carries chlorine and salt that slowly etch the surface. Sunscreen, suntan oil, and the occasional dropped drink leave stains that soak straight into porous concrete or clay. And every time you hose the deck down or someone climbs out of the pool, water erodes the sand in the joints — which is how that sand ends up in your pool filter and how weeds and ants colonize the gaps.
          </p>
          <p>
            A quality <strong>paver sealer</strong> solves all three problems at once. It creates a barrier that repels pool chemicals and stains, it stabilizes the polymeric sand in the joints so it stops washing out, and — with a wet-look product — it restores the rich, deep color that sun-faded pavers lose over time. The cost is modest: roughly <strong>$80-150 in sealer</strong> for an average deck, reapplied every 2-3 years. That's cheap insurance for a surface that can cost $15-30 per square foot to replace.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Why It's Worth It</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Avg. DIY sealing cost</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-3 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protection per coat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">90%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Less joint-sand washout</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">$15+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per sq ft to replace</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: Types -->
        <div id="types-of-sealers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Wet Look vs. Natural Look vs. Non-Slip: Which Finish Is Right?
          </h2>
          <p>
            Choosing the <strong>best paver sealer for a pool deck</strong> starts with deciding on a finish. Around water, this isn't just about looks — it's a safety decision. Here are the four categories that matter:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solvent-Based Wet Look Acrylics</h3>
          <p>
            These penetrate the paver and cure into a breathable film that deepens color into a rich, wet appearance. They're the most popular choice because they look fantastic and lock down joint sand. The catch around a pool: a glossy film can be slippery when wet, so you'll want to mix in an anti-slip additive for the splash zone. Durability is excellent — typically <strong>2-3 years</strong> between coats.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water-Based Acrylics</h3>
          <p>
            Lower odor and easier cleanup than solvent products, available in both wet-look and matte finishes. They're a great choice for covered or screened pool areas where solvent fumes are a problem. They generally need recoating a little sooner in intense sun, but the convenience and safety make them a strong pick for family decks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Penetrating (Silane/Siloxane) Sealers</h3>
          <p>
            These soak into the paver and protect from within, leaving no surface film and no change to the appearance or grip. They're the <strong>best non-slip paver sealer</strong> option because there's literally nothing on the surface to become slick. The trade-off: no color enhancement and no joint-sand lockdown. Ideal for safety-first, bare-foot decks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joint Stabilizing Sealers</h3>
          <p>
            Engineered specifically to harden the sand in paver joints into a firm matrix, stopping washout, weeds, and ant hills. They give a satin finish with modest color boost. Choose these if loose joint sand — not appearance — is your main complaint.
          </p>

