        <div id="how-to-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Seal a Pool Deck: The Complete DIY Guide
          </h2>
          <p>
            Sealing pavers around a pool is straightforward, but the wet environment makes a few steps non-negotiable. Here's the process that gives professional results:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Clean Thoroughly</h3>
          <p>
            Pressure wash the deck to remove sunscreen residue, body oils, algae, and grime. Treat any oil or rust stains with a dedicated paver cleaner. Pull weeds from the joints. The surface must be spotless — sealer bonds to clean pavers, not to the film of sunscreen and dirt that builds up poolside.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Re-Sand the Joints</h3>
          <p>
            Top up any joints that have lost sand with fresh polymeric sand, sweeping it in and compacting it. Film-forming and joint-stabilizing sealers will lock this sand in place, so getting the joints full now is what stops future washout into the pool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Let It Dry Completely</h3>
          <p>
            This is the step people skip and regret. Pavers must be <strong>bone dry</strong> — ideally 24-48 hours after washing with no rain. Sealing over trapped moisture is the number one cause of the milky white haze that ruins acrylic sealer jobs. Around a pool, also make sure splashing has stopped and the deck is fully dry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Add Anti-Slip to the Splash Zone</h3>
          <p>
            If you're using a glossy wet-look sealer, mix a micronized anti-slip additive into the sealer for the area within a few feet of the water's edge. This single step is what keeps a beautiful wet-look deck from becoming a slip hazard.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Apply Thin, Even Coats</h3>
          <p>
            Roll or spray the sealer in thin, even coats, working in manageable sections. Two thin coats beat one thick coat every time — flooding it on causes blushing and a tacky finish. Keep all traffic and water off the deck for the full cure time on the label, typically 24-48 hours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Non-Slip -->
        <div id="non-slip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Keeping Your Sealed Pool Deck Non-Slip
          </h2>
          <p>
            The single biggest worry homeowners have about sealing a pool deck is slipperiness — and rightly so. A glossy film on wet pavers can genuinely be hazardous. The good news is it's an easy problem to solve.
          </p>
          <p>
            The most reliable fix is a <strong>micronized polymer anti-slip additive</strong> mixed directly into the sealer. It's a fine, translucent powder that suspends in the wet sealer and creates microscopic texture in the cured film — invisible to the eye, but grippy underfoot. A small bag treats several gallons and costs only a few dollars. Add it to any wet-look or gloss sealer before you coat the splash zone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Alternatively, sidestep the issue entirely with a <strong>penetrating silane/siloxane sealer</strong> like the Foundation Armor SX5000. Because it leaves no surface film, the pavers feel exactly as grippy sealed as they did unsealed. You lose the wet-look color pop, but for a bare-foot family deck, many homeowners decide that's the right trade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Salt Water -->
        <div id="salt-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Salt Water Pool Considerations
          </h2>
          <p>
            Salt-water pools are increasingly popular, but salt is harder on pavers than chlorine. As salt water evaporates, it leaves crystals that can wick into porous pavers and cause surface spalling and efflorescence over time. If you have a salt pool, sealing isn't optional — it's preventive maintenance.
          </p>
          <p>
            Look for a sealer rated for <strong>salt and chloride resistance</strong>. Penetrating silane/siloxane sealers excel here because their DOT-grade chemistry was developed to protect concrete from road salt, and quality solvent acrylics also hold up well as long as they're applied to a clean, dry surface. Whichever you choose, reseal on schedule — a worn sealer offers no protection, and salt damage is far more expensive to fix than a maintenance coat.
          </p>
        </div>

